# Line endings are LF everywhere, on every platform, in the working tree.
#
# Without this, Git on Windows checks out text files with CRLF, and the Windows
# CI runner then reports every Go file as unformatted - gofmt wants LF and says
# so about all of them at once, which reads like a formatting catastrophe and is
# really a checkout setting. The build was red on six files that were correct.
#
# Go itself is unambiguous about this: gofmt writes LF, so anything that turns it
# into CRLF is changing gofmt's output.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Except the scripts that only ever run on Windows. CRLF is what that platform's
# text is, and these are the one thing here that is read and edited on it rather
# than merely built for it.
#
# No evidence LF broke anything: the installer's parse failure was an em dash
# read as CP-1252, and PowerShell accepts either ending. This is the last respect
# in which a file shipped to Windows still looked like it was written somewhere
# else, and it costs a line to remove.
#
# Safe against the rule above, which exists for gofmt, because gofmt has no
# opinion about .ps1.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binaries must not be touched at all. An icon is not text and a byte changed in
# one is a corrupted file, not a tidied one.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
